WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.160
A cluster gives us stars with approximately the

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.200
same age and distance, but different masses. Higher

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.460
mass members in this group are hotter and

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.200
more luminous. Common birth conditions make their different

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.760
stages useful evidence for stellar evolution.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.880
The luminous high mass members use their core

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:19.340
hydrogen sooner. They leave the main sequence and

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.640
change structure. Their points move away from the

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:24.560
upper main sequence as their envelopes expand and

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.260
cool. These are changes in the stars, not

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.140
stars moving across physical space.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.800
With increasing age, the surviving main sequence ends

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.100
at lower luminosity and cooler temperature. That bend

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.300
is the turnoff. Use it rather than the

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:41.800
brightest red giant. An old globular cluster can

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.760
still contain hot helium burning stars, so a

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.480
single blue star does not reset the cluster’s

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:47.960
age.
